        "original_text": "Let Phi and PhiPrime be real-valued functions on the real line. Assume Phi is continuous and has exact right derivative PhiPrime(u) within [u,infinity) at every u. If r Phi(u) <= PhiPrime(u) for every real u, then for every s <= t, Phi(s) <= Phi(t) exp(-r(t-s)). No sign condition on Phi or r is required. This is only the scalar endpoint comparison; concrete semigroup and curvature identities are outside its scope.",
        "text_sha256": "ca38e12b8c22b01d812e522f14b75a3eaa6a6b77d6a712331b11552426b352eb"
      },
      "lean": {
        "declaration": "AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.SemigroupDecay.backward_interpolation_contraction_of_growth",
        "file": "AutoSamplingTheory/TechnicalLemmas/FunctionalInequalities/BakryEmeryInterpolation.lean",
        "statement": "theorem backward_interpolation_contraction_of_growth\n    {interpolation interpolation' : ℝ → ℝ} {rate s t : ℝ}\n    (hcontinuous : Continuous interpolation)\n    (hderiv : ∀ u : ℝ,\n      HasDerivWithinAt interpolation (interpolation' u) (Ici u) u)\n    (hgrowth : ∀ u : ℝ,\n      rate * interpolation u ≤ interpolation' u)\n    (hst : s ≤ t) :\n    interpolation s ≤\n      interpolation t * Real.exp (-rate * (t - s))",
